    {
      "page_title": "Cache-Aside",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 1,
          "title": "Cache-Aside",
          "tagline": "the default read pattern"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "database",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "Lazy loading: app checks cache, on a miss reads DB, fills cache"
            },
            {
              "icon": "server",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "Repeated hot-row reads overload the slow database; cache stays disposable"
            },
            {
              "icon": "zap",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "Read-heavy data: user profiles, product pages, config, lookup tables"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How it works",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Step",
            "Command",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1",
              "GET user:42",
              "miss (nil)"
            ],
            [
              "2",
              "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id=42",
              "12 ms"
            ],
            [
              "3",
              "SET user:42 <json> EX 300",
              "cache filled"
            ],
            [
              "4",
              "GET user:42 (next read)",
              "hit, 0.4 ms"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 3,
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Only ==requested data== is cached",
            "A cache crash only **slows** requests, never loses data",
            "Simple to add to existing code"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "First request after a miss is slow (**cold start**)",
            "Data can go ==stale== between writes",
            "Every call site needs check-and-fill logic"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Interview takeaway:",
          "text": "The default read pattern: the app owns the cache logic.\nWrites update the DB then **delete** the key, not set it."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "database",
      "aside": "check cache first",
      "subtitle": "Lazy loading"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Write-Through",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 2,
          "title": "Write-Through",
          "tagline": "write both, then ack"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "zap",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "Every write hits cache and database together before the ack."
            },
            {
              "icon": "lightbulb",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "Cache-aside leaves a stale window; this keeps the cache current."
            },
            {
              "icon": "database",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "Data read right after writing: balances, sessions, inventory counts."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How it works",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Step",
            "Write path",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1",
              "`UPDATE stock SET qty=7 WHERE sku='A1'`",
              "DB ok"
            ],
            [
              "2",
              "`SET stock:A1 7`",
              "cache ok"
            ],
            [
              "3",
              "Client gets **OK**",
              "both succeeded"
            ],
            [
              "4",
              "`GET stock:A1`",
              "7, fresh hit"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Reads are always ==fresh== and fast",
            "No stale window after a write",
            "Cache is always warm for written keys"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Write latency = **sum** of both writes",
            "Data nobody reads still fills the cache",
            "Failure between writes needs retry or rollback"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Interview takeaway:",
          "text": "Trades slower writes for read consistency. Often paired with cache-aside for reads; it is not the same as write-back."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "database",
      "aside": "fresh reads!",
      "subtitle": "Sync write to both"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Write-Back",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 3,
          "title": "Write-Back",
          "tagline": "also called write-behind"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "database",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "Writes hit the cache only; flushed to the DB later in batches"
            },
            {
              "icon": "server",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "DB writes are the bottleneck; memory absorbs bursts for higher throughput"
            },
            {
              "icon": "zap",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "Counters, view counts, metrics, game scores, activity logs"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How it works",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "10,000 x INCR",
                "sub": "views:post9 in 1 s",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "Redis",
                "sub": "counter = 10000",
                "ink": "red"
              },
              {
                "text": "Worker, every 5 s",
                "sub": "flushes the batch",
                "ink": "purple"
              },
              {
                "text": "1 UPDATE",
                "sub": "SET views=views+10000",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**Benefits:** very low write latency",
            "==Coalesces== repeated writes to the same key",
            "Shields the database from spikes"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**Things to consider:** unflushed data is **lost** if the cache node dies",
            "Database lags behind the cache",
            "Needs a durable queue or AOF, plus a flush retry policy"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Interview takeaway:",
          "text": "Fastest writes, but ==weakest durability==.\nUse it only for data you can afford to lose a few seconds of."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "zap",
      "aside": "flush it later!",
      "subtitle": "Write-behind"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "TTL and Expiry",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 4,
          "title": "TTL and Expiry",
          "tagline": "a key's lifetime"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "zap",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "Per-key lifetime; after it, the cache treats the entry as gone"
            },
            {
              "icon": "shield-check",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "Bounds staleness and cache growth, even if invalidation fails"
            },
            {
              "icon": "lightbulb",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "Sessions 30m, OTP 5m, rate limits 60s, prices 5m"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How it works",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Step",
            "Command",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Store",
              "`SET otp:9876 481516 EX 300`",
              "OK"
            ],
            [
              "Check",
              "`TTL otp:9876`",
              "**297** s left"
            ],
            [
              "After 300 s",
              "`GET otp:9876`",
              "**nil**"
            ],
            [
              "Redis expiry",
              "lazy on access + sampler",
              "random keys purged"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==Automatic cleanup== of dead keys",
            "A hard **staleness bound**",
            "Cheap safety net behind explicit invalidation"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Too short: low **hit rate**",
            "Too long: **stale** reads",
            "Same TTL expires together: add ==jitter==, 300 s + random 0-60 s"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Interview takeaway:",
          "text": "TTL is the ==staleness budget==: pick it from how wrong the data may be.\nAlways add **jitter**."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "zap",
      "aside": "jitter it!",
      "subtitle": "Staleness budget"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Eviction Policies (LRU, LFU)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 5,
          "title": "Eviction Policies"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "target",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "Picks the key to drop: ==LRU== least recent, ==LFU== least frequent"
            },
            {
              "icon": "database",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "Memory is finite, so a full cache must discard to admit new"
            },
            {
              "icon": "server",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "Redis with maxmemory, in-process caches, CPU and page caches"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How it works",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Policy",
            "Cap 3 after A B C A",
            "Add D evicts"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "LRU",
              "order B, C, A",
              "**B** (oldest touch)"
            ],
            [
              "LFU",
              "A=2, B=1, C=1",
              "B or C, never A"
            ],
            [
              "Redis",
              "`maxmemory-policy`",
              "allkeys-lru / allkeys-lfu"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==LRU== is O(1): hash map plus doubly linked list",
            "LRU suits recency-biased, shifting traffic",
            "==LFU== shields long-term hot keys from one-off scans"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "One big scan pollutes LRU with cold keys",
            "LFU keeps formerly hot keys forever without counter decay",
            "Redis samples keys, so order is approximate",
            "`noeviction` returns errors when memory is full"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Interview takeaway:",
          "text": "Be able to code an O(1) LRU with a hash map and linked list.\nLFU wins on stable hot sets; LRU wins on shifting ones."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "database",
      "aside": "hot vs recent"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Cache Invalidation",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 6,
          "title": "Cache Invalidation",
          "tagline": "copies drift"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "trash",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "Remove or update cached entries when source data changes"
            },
            {
              "icon": "clock",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "A cache is a copy, and copies drift from the source"
            },
            {
              "icon": "tag",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "Product edits, profile updates, permission and price changes"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How it works",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Step",
            "Command",
            "Why"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1. Write",
              "UPDATE products SET price=19 WHERE id=7",
              "source of truth"
            ],
            [
              "2. Delete item",
              "DEL product:7",
              "next read refills"
            ],
            [
              "3. Delete list",
              "DEL category:shoes:page1",
              "list embeds price"
            ],
            [
              "4. Backstop",
              "EX 300 TTL on every key",
              "lost events heal"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==Bounded staleness== when you invalidate on write",
            "Targeted deletes keep the rest of the cache warm",
            "Versioned keys like `product:7:v12` avoid deletes entirely"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Derived and list keys are **easy to forget**",
            "Delete then update races can re-cache an old value",
            "Events can be lost, so keep a TTL fallback"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Interview takeaway:",
          "text": "Prefer **delete** over update on write, and track dependent keys.\nKeep a ==TTL== as the backstop."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "zap",
      "aside": "hardest problem!",
      "subtitle": "Keep copies honest"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Cache Stampede",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 7,
          "title": "Cache Stampede",
          "tagline": "thundering herd"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "zap",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "A hot key expires; many requests miss and hit the ==database== together"
            },
            {
              "icon": "lightbulb",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "Expiry is synchronized and the miss path is **unprotected**"
            },
            {
              "icon": "server",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "Homepage feed, trending lists, expensive aggregates"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How it works",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Case",
            "Action",
            "DB queries"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "No lock, 12:00:00",
              "2,000 req/s all miss, 300 ms each",
              "2,000"
            ],
            [
              "Winner",
              "`SET lock:home NX EX 10`, rebuilds",
              "1"
            ],
            [
              "1,999 waiters",
              "wait 50 ms, re-read `home:feed`",
              "0"
            ],
            [
              "After rebuild",
              "all reads HIT the cache",
              "0"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1,
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Lock or single-flight turns ==N queries into 1==",
            "TTL jitter spreads expiries apart",
            "Stale-while-revalidate serves the old value during refresh"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Locks need a **timeout**, or a crashed rebuilder blocks everyone",
            "Waiters need a retry cap",
            "Probabilistic early refresh needs tuning"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Interview takeaway:",
          "text": "Name three fixes: lock or single-flight, TTL jitter,\nand early or background refresh."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "zap",
      "aside": "one key, one rebuild",
      "subtitle": "Thundering herd"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "CDN Caching",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 8,
          "title": "CDN Caching",
          "tagline": "headers decide everything"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "cloudfront",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "Content cached on edge servers near users, set by HTTP headers"
            },
            {
              "icon": "zap",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "Oceans cost ~150 ms latency; origin bandwidth is expensive"
            },
            {
              "icon": "server",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "Static assets, images, video segments, public APIs, cacheable HTML"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How it works",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Request",
            "Cache-Control",
            "X-Cache"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`app.4f9a1c.js` 1st",
              "public, max-age=31536000, immutable",
              "**MISS**"
            ],
            [
              "`app.4f9a1c.js` later",
              "same header, cached 1 year",
              "**HIT**"
            ],
            [
              "`/pricing` HTML 1st",
              "s-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=300",
              "**MISS**"
            ],
            [
              "`/pricing` within 60 s",
              "served from the edge",
              "**HIT**"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Lower latency ==worldwide==",
            "Origin offload, often **90%+** hit ratio",
            "Absorbs traffic spikes and DDoS"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Purging is slow or costly: use ==hashed filenames==",
            "Keep **cookies** out of cacheable responses",
            "`Vary` headers can fragment the cache"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Interview takeaway:",
          "text": "Control the CDN with `Cache-Control`.\nImmutable hashed assets, short `s-maxage` for dynamic pages."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "network",
      "aside": "cache near users",
      "subtitle": "Edge caching"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Distributed Caches",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "number": 9,
          "title": "Distributed Caches",
          "tagline": "Redis, Memcached"
        },
        {
          "type": "labelrows",
          "rows": [
            {
              "icon": "network",
              "label": "What it is:",
              "text": "One cache shared by many app servers, spread across nodes."
            },
            {
              "icon": "lightbulb",
              "label": "Why it exists:",
              "text": "Per-process caches duplicate data, diverge, and vanish on each deploy."
            },
            {
              "icon": "zap",
              "label": "Used for:",
              "text": "Sessions, rate limiting, leaderboards, pub/sub, shared hot data."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How it works",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "tables",
          "spec": {
            "tables": [
              {
                "title": "slot = CRC16(key) mod 16384",
                "headers": [
                  "Node / key",
                  "Rule",
                  "Maps to"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "Node A",
                    "slots 0–5460",
                    "replica A′"
                  ],
                  [
                    "Node B",
                    "slots 5461–10922",
                    "replica B′"
                  ],
                  [
                    "Node C",
                    "slots 10923–16383",
                    "replica C′"
                  ],
                  [
                    "==user:42==",
                    "CRC16 mod 16384",
                    "**one** slot, one node"
                  ],
                  [
                    "Memcached",
                    "consistent hashing",
                    "client picks ring node"
                  ]
                ]
              }
            ],
            "arrow": false
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Horizontal scaling of **memory** and throughput",
            "One shared view of the data for every server",
            "Redis adds replication, persistence, hash, sorted set, stream"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Memcached: multi-threaded, simple, but strings only, no persistence",
            "Redis is mostly single-threaded per shard",
            "A network hop costs ~0.5 ms",
            "==Hot keys== overload one node"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Interview takeaway:",
          "text": "Redis for data structures and persistence; Memcached for plain, high-throughput key-value caching. Know how keys map to nodes: hash slots or a hash ring."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "server",
      "aside": "key → node",
      "subtitle": "Redis vs Memcached"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Cache Consistency",
      "blocks": [
        {
